1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,000
December 2008.

2
00:00:01,001 --> 00:00:05,645
A fishing boat slips out of a small harbor
on Korea's west coast.

3
00:00:05,646 --> 00:00:11,863
According to investigators, one of the passengers is
the most wanted man in the country.

4
00:00:12,414 --> 00:00:18,907
Behind him, tens of thousands of people are
discovering that their savings are gone.

5
00:00:18,908 --> 00:00:23,825
Retirees. Homemakers. Small shop owners.
Families who had been promised

6
00:00:23,826 --> 00:00:29,234
that a simple health machine would pay
them every single day.

7
00:00:30,235 --> 00:00:35,358
Three years later, the news arrived from
China. He was dead. Heart attack.

8
00:00:35,359 --> 00:00:39,306
There was a death certificate. There was a
funeral video.

9
00:00:39,307 --> 00:00:41,383
And almost nobody believed it.

10
00:00:41,634 --> 00:00:45,234
This is the story of Cho Hee-pal. How the
scheme worked,

11
00:00:45,235 --> 00:00:49,489
who let it run, and why a country still
argues about one funeral.

12
00:00:57,123 --> 00:01:02,703
The story begins in Daegu, a big industrial
city in South Korea's southeast.

13
00:01:02,704 --> 00:01:05,650
Daegu is known as a conservative,

14
00:01:05,651 --> 00:01:10,071
tight-knit place. Neighbors know
neighbors. Word of mouth matters.

15
00:01:10,072 --> 00:01:12,750
That would turn out to be important.

16
00:01:13,301 --> 00:01:19,242
The man at the center of this story was
Cho Hee-pal, born in 1957.

17
00:01:19,493 --> 00:01:23,907
In October 2004, he set up a company in
Daegu called BMC.

18
00:01:23,908 --> 00:01:28,631
And it started selling a deal that sounded
almost too simple.

19
00:01:29,182 --> 00:01:34,504
Korea is famous for its public bathhouses
and saunas, called jjimjilbang.

20
00:01:34,505 --> 00:01:36,327
The pitch went like this.

21
00:01:36,578 --> 00:01:41,071
You buy a health device. A massage
machine, a pelvic-correction bed,

22
00:01:41,072 --> 00:01:48,015
even a karaoke machine. One unit cost 4.4 million won,
roughly four thousand dollars at the time.

23
00:01:48,016 --> 00:01:53,874
You never take it home. The company rents your
machine out to bathhouses and clinics.

24
00:01:54,125 --> 00:01:55,905
And you get paid every day.

25
00:01:56,456 --> 00:02:00,948
Let's walk through it the way an investor
would have seen it.

26
00:02:00,949 --> 00:02:04,925
Step one. You pay 4.4 million won. That's
one machine.

27
00:02:05,476 --> 00:02:09,722
Step two. According to Korean reporting
from the time,

28
00:02:09,723 --> 00:02:17,743
about 35,000 won lands in your account almost every business
day. That's around thirty dollars, every day.

29
00:02:18,294 --> 00:02:21,427
Step three. After about 166 payments,

30
00:02:21,428 --> 00:02:26,127
roughly eight months, you've received
about 5.8 million won.

31
00:02:26,128 --> 00:02:29,762
Your money back, plus 1.4 million won on
top.

32
00:02:30,313 --> 00:02:36,220
The court that later tried the group's number two described
the promise as a fixed 35 percent a year.

33
00:02:36,221 --> 00:02:41,065
Run the numbers on eight months, and it
works out closer to 48 percent annualized.

34
00:02:41,066 --> 00:02:44,965
A normal Korean bank deposit at the time
paid around five percent.

35
00:02:46,066 --> 00:02:48,963
The daily payment was the hook. The ladder
was the engine.

36
00:02:49,214 --> 00:02:55,193
According to a December 2008 report by the
Korean news magazine Sisa IN,

37
00:02:55,194 --> 00:03:03,474
investors were organized into ranks, like a company. Department head.
Bureau chief. Division head. Group leader. Executive director.

38
00:03:04,025 --> 00:03:08,491
To become a department head, the report
said, you had to run two

39
00:03:08,492 --> 00:03:13,302
lines of investors below you, and each
line had to bring in twelve machines.

40
00:03:13,303 --> 00:03:17,875
Twenty-four machines. Around 100 million
won of other people's money.

41
00:03:18,126 --> 00:03:21,336
Only then did the company pay you a salary
and bonuses.

42
00:03:21,737 --> 00:03:27,909
You are no longer just an investor. You are a
recruiter. Your income depends on your sister,

43
00:03:27,910 --> 00:03:33,356
your old classmates, the women from your church, the
man who runs the shop downstairs.

44
00:03:33,907 --> 00:03:37,215
But there was a basic problem underneath
all of it.

45
00:03:37,216 --> 00:03:41,238
Machines rented to saunas could never earn
that kind of money.

46
00:03:41,239 --> 00:03:45,099
According to prosecutors, the "rental
income" being paid out

47
00:03:45,100 --> 00:03:48,960
was largely new investors' money, recycled
to earlier ones.

48
00:03:49,686 --> 00:03:51,579
The money did arrive. At first.

49
00:03:51,580 --> 00:03:55,244
Early investors were paid on time. They
told friends. They

50
00:03:55,245 --> 00:03:58,910
told family. And they earned commissions
for bringing them in.

51
00:03:59,461 --> 00:04:04,655
From Daegu, the network spread down to
Busan and the southern coast,

52
00:04:04,656 --> 00:04:09,417
up through the Chungcheong provinces, and
into Seoul, Gyeonggi and Incheon.

53
00:04:09,418 --> 00:04:15,405
By late 2008, Korean media were counting around
fifty sales centers across the country.

54
00:04:15,956 --> 00:04:17,252
So who signed up?

55
00:04:17,253 --> 00:04:20,303
Not bankers. Not professional investors.

56
00:04:20,554 --> 00:04:26,714
In the Seoul area, the same 2008 report
said, about seven in ten investors were

57
00:04:26,715 --> 00:04:32,054
homemakers in their forties and fifties. Later
reporting described large numbers of retirees,

58
00:04:32,055 --> 00:04:39,448
people who had lost jobs, and families still recovering from
the Asian financial crisis of the late 1990s.

59
00:04:39,849 --> 00:04:46,249
You put in your money. And then, every single day, a
payment lands in your account. Like clockwork.

60
00:04:46,250 --> 00:04:50,129
After a few weeks, the doubts fade. After
a few months,

61
00:04:50,130 --> 00:04:53,304
you're the one telling your sister she's
missing out.

62
00:04:53,555 --> 00:04:58,856
That daily payment wasn't just a return.
It was proof. Or it looked like proof.

63
00:05:00,057 --> 00:05:02,515
Here's what makes this story hard to
watch.

64
00:05:02,516 --> 00:05:07,203
The scheme did not hide in the dark for four
years. The warnings are on the record.

65
00:05:07,754 --> 00:05:14,457
According to police and prosecutors, police began
looking into the group as early as 2006.

66
00:05:14,458 --> 00:05:18,187
In May 2007, a police station in Miryang,

67
00:05:18,188 --> 00:05:24,249
in South Gyeongsang Province, opened a formal
investigation into companies linked to Cho.

68
00:05:24,800 --> 00:05:30,007
And then something happened that a court
would later describe in detail.

69
00:05:30,008 --> 00:05:34,934
In August 2007, Kang Tae-yong, Cho's
right-hand man, handed fifty million won,

70
00:05:34,935 --> 00:05:42,736
twice, to a police sergeant at the Daegu police agency. A
sergeant who was working on the Cho case.

71
00:05:42,987 --> 00:05:46,258
A district court later found that the
sergeant,

72
00:05:46,259 --> 00:05:51,167
in return, dragged out the investigation and gave the
group "investigative convenience."

73
00:05:51,568 --> 00:05:55,201
In April 2008, according to Sisa IN,

74
00:05:55,202 --> 00:06:03,506
Korea's financial regulator, the Financial Supervisory Service, flagged
two of the group's companies for illegal deposit-taking,

75
00:06:03,507 --> 00:06:07,659
and notified police in Daegu, Busan and
Incheon.

76
00:06:07,910 --> 00:06:12,383
In September 2008, victims began filing
complaints.

77
00:06:12,384 --> 00:06:14,576
By then, it was too late.

78
00:06:15,777 --> 00:06:21,404
In the autumn of 2008, the global financial crisis
was shaking markets everywhere.

79
00:06:21,405 --> 00:06:24,739
In Korea, investigators were finally
closing in.

80
00:06:24,740 --> 00:06:29,324
In October 2008 the payments stopped, and
the companies collapsed.

81
00:06:29,725 --> 00:06:31,735
Cho was put on the wanted list.

82
00:06:31,736 --> 00:06:35,304
Some victims reportedly photocopied the
wanted posters themselves

83
00:06:35,305 --> 00:06:38,872
and carried them to ports around the
country.

84
00:06:39,123 --> 00:06:41,835
But according to investigators, on
December 9th,

85
00:06:41,836 --> 00:06:48,035
2008, he boarded a fishing boat from a
small port in Taean, on the west coast.

86
00:06:48,036 --> 00:06:52,986
At sea, he reportedly switched to another
vessel and crossed to China.

87
00:06:53,537 --> 00:06:59,459
Years later, prosecutors and the courts put a
number on what the group had collected.

88
00:06:59,460 --> 00:07:02,844
About 5 trillion won. Roughly 4 billion
dollars.

89
00:07:02,845 --> 00:07:04,677
From around 70,000 people.

90
00:07:05,228 --> 00:07:10,491
That 5 trillion figure counts money that
went round and round,

91
00:07:10,492 --> 00:07:12,883
as people reinvested their "profits."

92
00:07:12,884 --> 00:07:15,007
Prosecutors estimated the real,

93
00:07:15,008 --> 00:07:20,849
unrecovered losses at about 840 billion
won. Around 700 million dollars.

94
00:07:21,100 --> 00:07:25,499
And they found that the group had stashed
away about 290 billion won.

95
00:07:25,500 --> 00:07:30,727
Korean media started calling it the biggest fraud
since the founding of the nation.

96
00:07:31,728 --> 00:07:34,750
Before we go on, keep two things in mind.

97
00:07:34,751 --> 00:07:41,089
One: a cheque, handed over in a hotel coffee shop,
to a man whose job was to catch Cho.

98
00:07:41,090 --> 00:07:44,131
Two: a single piece of paper from China,

99
00:07:44,132 --> 00:07:48,314
dated ten days before the death it was
supposed to record.

100
00:07:48,565 --> 00:07:54,986
The first explains how he got away. The second is why so
many people still don't believe he's gone.

101
00:07:56,187 --> 00:08:00,502
Here's the part that turned a fraud case
into a national scandal.

102
00:08:00,503 --> 00:08:05,815
How did one man run a scheme this big, for
this long, and then simply sail away?

103
00:08:06,816 --> 00:08:11,202
We've already met the first one. The
police sergeant on the case.

104
00:08:11,203 --> 00:08:16,668
The court found he took 200 million won
from Kang in total. Two payments in 2007.

105
00:08:16,669 --> 00:08:23,552
And a third, on October 31st, 2008, at a hotel in Daegu,
while the group's offices were being searched.

106
00:08:23,803 --> 00:08:26,855
A district court sentenced him to eight
years in prison.

107
00:08:27,406 --> 00:08:29,186
The second was more senior.

108
00:08:29,187 --> 00:08:34,793
In October 2008, he headed the violent-crime unit at
the Daegu regional police agency.

109
00:08:35,044 --> 00:08:42,049
According to the courts, on October 30th, 2008, he met
Cho in person, in the coffee shop of

110
00:08:42,050 --> 00:08:49,055
a hotel in Daegu, and received 900 million won as a bank
cheque. That's around 800 thousand dollars.

111
00:08:49,306 --> 00:08:52,145
He was sentenced to ten years at first,

112
00:08:52,146 --> 00:08:56,759
reduced to nine on appeal. The Supreme
Court confirmed it in December 2016.

113
00:08:57,310 --> 00:09:00,676
By the time the investigation was done,

114
00:09:00,677 --> 00:09:05,487
prosecutors said eight police and prosecution
officials had faced punishment.

115
00:09:05,488 --> 00:09:08,377
For the victims, this changed everything.

116
00:09:08,378 --> 00:09:13,876
The people who were supposed to catch him,
some of them, had been paid by him.

117
00:09:15,077 --> 00:09:16,422
So where was Cho?

118
00:09:16,423 --> 00:09:23,309
According to investigators, he moved around China,
reportedly living under another name.

119
00:09:23,310 --> 00:09:27,584
Then, in May 2012, Korean police made an
announcement.

120
00:09:28,135 --> 00:09:34,529
Cho Hee-pal, they said, had died in China in
December 2011, of an acute heart attack.

121
00:09:34,530 --> 00:09:39,459
Police said they had found Chinese
emergency-care records and a

122
00:09:39,460 --> 00:09:44,883
death certificate while searching the
homes of his family and associates.

123
00:09:45,434 --> 00:09:50,271
They pointed to a video of his funeral,
reportedly filmed by family members.

124
00:09:50,272 --> 00:09:55,505
According to Korean reporting at the time,
he had been cremated in China within days,

125
00:09:55,506 --> 00:09:59,693
and his ashes brought back to Korea before
the end of December.

126
00:10:00,319 --> 00:10:01,831
The public didn't buy it.

127
00:10:01,832 --> 00:10:04,373
Cremation meant there was no body to test.

128
00:10:04,374 --> 00:10:10,667
Even the police kept him on the wanted list, saying the death
had not been confirmed one hundred percent.

129
00:10:11,218 --> 00:10:13,255
Remember that piece of paper?

130
00:10:13,256 --> 00:10:17,953
In 2015, victims' groups pointed to a
Chinese cremation document that,

131
00:10:17,954 --> 00:10:24,359
according to Korean reporting, was issued ten days
before the date of death it recorded.

132
00:10:24,760 --> 00:10:27,001
Then, on October 10th, 2015,

133
00:10:27,002 --> 00:10:32,832
Chinese police arrested Kang Tae-yong, the
right-hand man, in the city of Wuxi.

134
00:10:32,833 --> 00:10:36,227
On December 16th, he was flown back to
Korea.

135
00:10:36,228 --> 00:10:42,867
Arriving at the prosecutors' office in Daegu, he was
asked by reporters if Cho was alive.

136
00:10:43,118 --> 00:10:45,706
He said he had seen Cho dead with his own
eyes.

137
00:10:46,257 --> 00:10:52,900
In June 2016, after a two-year reinvestigation,
prosecutors announced their conclusion.

138
00:10:52,901 --> 00:10:58,691
They said Cho collapsed after a night of
drinking at a hotel in Weihai,

139
00:10:58,692 --> 00:11:05,723
in China's Shandong Province, and died at a hospital in
the early hours of December 19th, 2011.

140
00:11:05,974 --> 00:11:12,856
They said they had consistent testimony from people who were
with him, and from fourteen people at the funeral.

141
00:11:12,857 --> 00:11:18,800
They said a DNA test on hair matched Cho, and that the
funeral video showed no signs of editing.

142
00:11:19,051 --> 00:11:22,037
They said they had found no evidence that
he was alive.

143
00:11:22,588 --> 00:11:28,046
Because he was dead, they said, he could not be
charged. The case against him was closed.

144
00:11:28,047 --> 00:11:33,008
Some victims' groups rejected that conclusion and
said they would keep searching.

145
00:11:33,634 --> 00:11:35,241
So, is Cho Hee-pal dead?

146
00:11:35,242 --> 00:11:38,658
The official answer from Korean
authorities is yes.

147
00:11:38,659 --> 00:11:44,622
The doubts have never been proven. But for many
victims, they have never gone away either.

148
00:11:45,823 --> 00:11:52,356
With Cho officially dead, Kang Tae-yong became the
highest-ranking member of the group ever to stand trial.

149
00:11:52,357 --> 00:11:54,637
Prosecutors asked for life in prison.

150
00:11:55,188 --> 00:11:59,064
In January 2017, a Daegu court found that,

151
00:11:59,065 --> 00:12:07,304
from June 2006 to October 2008, together with Cho, he
had collected around 5 trillion won from

152
00:12:07,305 --> 00:12:13,605
roughly 70,000 people through unlicensed
deposit-taking, promising a fixed 35 percent a year.

153
00:12:13,856 --> 00:12:18,690
It convicted him of fraud, embezzlement,
bribery, and hiding criminal proceeds.

154
00:12:18,691 --> 00:12:24,750
The court also found that he had paid the police sergeant
on the case for investigation information.

155
00:12:25,151 --> 00:12:28,969
Prosecutors charged around seventy people
in total over the scheme.

156
00:12:28,970 --> 00:12:33,017
But the man whose name was on the whole
thing would never face a judge.

157
00:12:34,218 --> 00:12:36,325
But what about the victims?

158
00:12:36,326 --> 00:12:42,101
Prosecutors said about 95 billion won had
been tracked down and recovered.

159
00:12:42,102 --> 00:12:45,536
Against the losses, that's a small
fraction.

160
00:12:45,537 --> 00:12:52,405
Spread across 70,000 people, it's roughly 1.4 million
won each. About a thousand dollars.

161
00:12:53,281 --> 00:12:55,294
Here's the uncomfortable part.

162
00:12:55,295 --> 00:12:59,388
Cho's scheme collapsed in 2008. The idea
behind it never did.

163
00:12:59,939 --> 00:13:05,198
One. A fixed, guaranteed return, far above
what any bank pays.

164
00:13:05,199 --> 00:13:07,476
Two. Payments that come often,

165
00:13:07,477 --> 00:13:13,853
daily or weekly, so the scheme feels real
long before it can be checked.

166
00:13:13,854 --> 00:13:17,544
Three. A product story you can't easily
verify.

167
00:13:17,545 --> 00:13:21,234
A machine in a sauna you'll never visit.

168
00:13:21,485 --> 00:13:27,139
Four. Bonuses for bringing in others, so
victims become recruiters.

169
00:13:27,140 --> 00:13:31,317
And five. Time. Every month that nobody
stops it,

170
00:13:31,318 --> 00:13:37,353
the hole gets deeper, and the people
inside it get more people involved.

171
00:13:38,454 --> 00:13:42,459
Officially, the story ended in a hotel
room in China in 2011.

172
00:13:42,460 --> 00:13:46,794
For many of the people who lost
everything, it never ended at all.

173
00:13:47,795 --> 00:13:51,284
The scheme didn't run on machines. It ran
on trust.

174
00:13:51,285 --> 00:13:55,732
Trust in a daily payment. Trust in the
friend who brought you in.

175
00:13:55,733 --> 00:14:00,454
And trust that if something was wrong, the
authorities would stop it.

176
00:14:01,005 --> 00:14:05,538
So here's the question to ask, every time:
if a business pays you

177
00:14:05,539 --> 00:14:10,072
more than any bank ever could, where is
that money really coming from?

178
00:14:10,623 --> 00:14:12,720
Next time: another country,

179
00:14:12,721 --> 00:14:18,490
another promise that sounded safe. And
another fortune that simply disappeared.

180
00:14:19,041 --> 00:14:20,844
Subscribe so you don't miss it.

181
00:14:20,845 --> 00:14:26,023
And tell me in the comments: have you ever been
offered a deal that paid every single day?
